**(i) Plate tectonics (4 marks).**

The theory that the **lithosphere (the crust and the uppermost, rigid mantle) is broken into a few large and several small rigid plates** that **move relative to one another over the weaker asthenosphere** at about 1 to 10 cm per year. The movement is driven mainly by **mantle convection, ridge push and slab pull**. Plate **boundaries** are **divergent (constructive)** (mid-ocean ridges and rifts), **convergent (destructive)** (subduction zones, collision belts) and **transform (conservative)** (plates slide past each other). Most **earthquakes, volcanoes, fold mountains and ocean trenches** occur along these boundaries.

**(ii) Continental drift (4 marks).**

The hypothesis of **Alfred Wegener (1912)** that the continents were once joined in one supercontinent, **Pangaea**, which **broke up** (from about 200 Ma) and the continents **drifted apart** to their present positions. Evidence:

1. the **jigsaw fit** of the coastlines (South America and Africa, best on the continental shelf);
2. **matching rock types, structures and mountain belts** on opposite sides of the Atlantic;
3. **identical fossils** (the plant *Glossopteris*, the reptiles *Mesosaurus* and *Lystrosaurus*) on widely separated continents;
4. **Permo-Carboniferous glacial deposits and striations** in southern continents, and coal in cold regions (palaeoclimate evidence).

Wegener could **not explain the force** that moves continents, so the idea was rejected until the discovery of sea-floor spreading.

**(iii) Seafloor spreading (4 marks).**

The idea (**Harry Hess, early 1960s**) that **new oceanic crust forms continuously at mid-ocean ridges**, where plates diverge and **basaltic magma rises, cools and solidifies**; the new crust is then **carried away on both sides** of the ridge, and the oceans widen. Evidence:

1. **symmetrical magnetic stripes (reversals) on both sides of the ridge** (Vine and Matthews);
2. the **age of the ocean floor increases away from the ridge**, and it is nowhere older than about 180 Ma;
3. **sediment on the ocean floor is thin near the ridge and thickens away from it**;
4. **high heat flow, shallow earthquakes and volcanism along the ridge**.

The old crust is destroyed by **subduction** at the **trenches**, so the Earth does not grow in size.
